WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.200
(bright music)

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.850
- [Narrator] Breasts come in
different shapes and sizes,

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:09.120
but there's one thing
they all have in common.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.540
They are unique to humans.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.790
More than 5,000 mammalian
species inhabit this planet,

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:17.600
yet homo sapiens are the only life forms

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.080
with permanent breasts.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.430
Some may call this human anomaly sexy,

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.640
but it also begs the question,

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.340
why are human breasts so big,

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.389
and were they an evolutionary mistake?

00:00:29.389 --> 00:00:32.780
(text clicking)

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:35.460
Every other mammal
develops temporary breasts

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.760
during ovulation and, or nursing.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.120
Basically their purpose
is to produce milk,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.400
so once the milk is gone,
the breasts disappear.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:45.810
But this isn't the case for female humans

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:49.290
whose breasts form during
puberty, not pregnancy.

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:53.170
So at some point in our
evolution, something changed.

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:54.240
But what?

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.160
For example, in 1987,
biologist Tim Caro explored

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:00.920
seven existing theories on this subject.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:03.070
One was that breasts allowed mothers

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.340
to nurse newborns from their hip,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.910
giving them more mobility to multitask.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:10.250
But it doesn't explain
why breasts stick around

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.490
after the nursing stage is over.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:15.240
Perhaps the most popular
idea was first proposed

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.160
by Charles Darwin and later
explored by zoologist,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.420
Desmond Morris, in his
1967 book, The Naked Ape.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:23.850
In it, Morris suggests
that breasts evolved

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.080
as a sex symbol, to replace
the swelling rear end

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.650
of other female primates during ovulation.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.150
Once our ancestors
starting walking upright,

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.220
the sexual organs were no
longer as obvious to spot,

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.030
so males had no way of knowing

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.020
when a female was sexually mature,

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.200
and breasts may have formed as a result.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.740
This theory would at least explain

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.660
why women's chests swell during puberty,

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:48.730
but it still can't explain why they

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.680
stick around after menopause.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.270
Let's take a closer look
at the human breast.

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.840
The big difference is
that it contains more fat

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:57.620
than other female mammals.

00:01:57.620 --> 00:01:59.340
The fat fills out the breast tissue,

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:03.240
giving it shape, sort of
like milk, but permanent.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.310
Human breasts can become so large

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.600
it can cause back and chest pain.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.440
This is why many women
get breast reductions.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.000
More than 61,000 received
a breast reduction

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.760
in 2016, in the US alone.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.690
But breasts aren't only
uncomfortable for some,

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.750
they can also be deadly.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.590
Breast cancer is the number one cause

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:25.660
of cancer related deaths
in women, world wide.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:28.800
It effects roughly one and a
half million women each year

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.180
and it killed 570,000 of them in 2015.

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.950
Yet as far as scientists can tell,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.490
breast cancer is not common
among other primates.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:43.140
This could be because the risk
of cancer increases with age,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:45.050
and other primates don't live long enough

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:47.610
to develop breast cancer, or perhaps,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.210
it could have something to do

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:51.670
with the permanent breast tissue itself.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.860
- [Dr. Callahan] Cancer is more common

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:55.900
in rapidly dividing tissue.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.240
Every time cells are born and die,

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.510
there is an opportunity
within the cell cycle

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.330
to make mistakes in repairing DNA

00:03:07.330 --> 00:03:10.650
and essentially, a cell with mistakes

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.430
can become a cancerous cell.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.500
Breast tissue divides at a rapid pace,

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.320
so there's a greater
opportunity to make mistakes.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.000
- [Narrator] That may explain
why removing both breasts

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.000
reduces a woman's risk
of breast cancer by 95%.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.340
Of course breasts have
established their place

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:31.560
in human culture and society.

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.330
They can make women feel
wanted, liberated, or empowered.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:37.520
And they've helped build entire empires

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:40.490
based simply on their power to attract.

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.570
It looks like, for better or worse,

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.120
human breasts are here to stay.

00:03:45.134 --> 00:03:47.801
(bright music)

